5. User stories and acceptance criteria
Each acceptance criterion describes an expected result, not an executed test. Dates in
examples use 2026.
US-01
As a student, I want to sign in, so that I can use the library with my own account.
AC-01a: Given an active student account and correct credentials, sign-in opens the student
dashboard. Wrong credentials or an inactive account are rejected.
AC-01b: Inspect the stored account and sign-in logs: only a salted adaptive hash is stored,
and no plaintext password appears in either logs or screens.
US-02
As a student, I want to search for books and check their availability, so that I know whether I
can borrow them.
AC-02a: Search a sample catalog using a known title, author and ISBN separately; each
search returns its matching book. An unmatched query shows a no-results message.
AC-02b: A book with 3 active copies (1 on loan, 1 held, 1 free) shows the correct title,
author, ISBN, total of 3 and available count of 1.
AC-02c: With 1,000 books and 20 concurrent users, at least 95 of 100 searches finish
within 2 seconds.
AC-02d: At least 4 of 5 first-time users find a specified book and its available count within
60 seconds without help.
US-03
As a librarian, I want to maintain book and copy records, so that the catalog stays correct.
AC-03a: Add a book and two copies with distinct IDs, then edit its title; the updated title
appears in the catalog and a duplicate copy ID is rejected.
AC-03b: Retiring a free copy succeeds and removes it from active availability; retiring an
on-loan or held copy is rejected.
US-04
As a librarian, I want to manage membership records, so that only eligible members can
borrow books.
AC-04a: Register one student and one faculty member with distinct university IDs; both
records can be viewed, and a duplicate ID is rejected.
AC-04b: Deactivate a member, verify that the status changes to inactive, and reactivate the
member; the status returns to active.
US-05
As a librarian, I want to issue books with the correct due date, so that loans can be tracked.
AC-05a: On 25 November, issue a free copy to an eligible student and another to eligible
faculty; due dates are 9 December and 23 December respectively under the proposed rules.
Each loan stores the four required fields and the copy becomes unavailable.
AC-05b: Attempt an issue to an inactive member, a student with 3 open loans, a member
with an overdue loan, or a member trying to take another member’s held copy; each attempt
is rejected with a reason and no loan is created.
AC-05c: Two simultaneous requests for the same free copy result in exactly one successful
issue and one open loan; no active hold remains on that copy
